      { question: "Are clones better than seeds for Maine's short season?", answer: "Yes for photoperiods in northern and central Maine. A clone transplanted in late May is in mid-vegetative growth by early June, vs. a seed started at the same time which is in early-veg by mid-June. The 3-5 week calendar head start is the difference between a 7-week photoperiod finishing before Caribou's Sep 25 first frost and not finishing. Autoflower clones are a category error (Maine Clone Co: 'impossible to keep an autoflowering mother'). For autoflowers, buy seeds." },
      { question: "How do I know if a cannabis clone is healthy?", answer: "Inspect roots: white, fibrous, no smell. Inspect stems: no discoloration, no cankers. Inspect leaves: no spots, no pest webbing, no curling, no 'tacoing' (leaf edges curling upward — a russet mite sign). For HLVd: most infected plants are asymptomatic until flower, so visual inspection alone is insufficient. Ask the vendor for RT-PCR or qPCR mother-plant testing protocols. A vendor who can't answer is a red flag. Nova Analytic Labs and other Maine testing facilities offer HLVd RT-PCR for a fee." },
