      { question: "What is HLVd and how do I avoid it?", answer: "Hop latent viroid (HLVd) is a 256-nucleotide circular RNA pathogen that causes 'dudding' disease in cannabis, with yield losses of 30-50% per the Adkar-Purushothama 2023 Viruses review. It is primarily mechanically transmitted (dirty tools, hands, gloves, shared cloners) and is mostly asymptomatic until the flowering stage. To avoid: buy from a vendor who tests mothers by RT-PCR or qPCR; quarantine new clones 7-14 days (Adkar-Purushothama recommends 30 days with week-3 RT-PCR test); inspect roots and leaves; use first-generation mothers when possible (Maine Seedlings' practice); do not integrate untested clones into an existing grow." },
      { question: "Are autoflower clones available in Maine?", answer: "No. As Maine Clone Company states: 'it's impossible to keep an autoflowering mother as it would flower and die even under 24 hours of light.' The autoflowering trait is day-neutral — the plant begins flowering at age 3-4 weeks regardless of light cycle. There is no benefit to a 4-week-old autoflower clone vs. a seed started at the same time, because the clock has already started ticking. A serious autoflower Maine grower buys seeds." }
